{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Ice-Free Summer: Cyanobacteria Bloom the North Pole",
    "date": "2026-10-03",
    "lesson": "As Arctic sea ice retreats earlier and farther each summer, patches of open water once perpetually capped by ice now sit exposed to 24-hour polar daylight. Picocyanobacteria (Synechococcus-like cells) that used to be minor players under thick ice now bloom explosively in this new open-water niche, while diatom chains that depend on ice-edge stability struggle as the melt outpaces them. The lesson: the Arctic's new phototroph winners are not the classic ice algae but opportunistic free-floating cyanobacteria exploiting a longer, brighter, but also more unstable growing season. Grazers and phage still operate, but the real driver here is continuous light meeting a very cold, nutrient-thin surface layer.",
    "citation": "Cf. Lewis et al. 2020 Science 'Changes in phytoplankton concentration now drive increased Arctic Ocean primary production'; Ardyna & Arrigo 2020 Nature Climate Change.",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Arctic Ocean summer ice-free surface waters, high latitude, continuous daylight bloom of picocyanobacteria"
  },
